// The provider-AGNOSTIC vocabulary of the AI abstraction: the conversation history shape and a model
// step's outcome. Each provider module maps these to/from its own wire format; the orchestration
// (`ai`) and apps speak only this vocabulary. No provider details leak in here — that is
// the whole point of the abstraction.
//
// EVERY vocabulary element here is a CLOSED KATARI TYPE, never a wire token. That rule is what this
// module is for: a `role` spelled as a string carries whichever provider's token was typed first, and
// the provider whose token it happens to be then maps it with the identity function while the others
// translate — so the abstraction quietly becomes that provider's wire model and the next provider
// inherits a vocabulary shaped for someone else's API. A sum has no such favourite: every provider
// matches it, every provider writes its own tokens, and a token a provider invents cannot appear here
// because there is nowhere to put it.

@"Whose turn this is: the CALLER's side of the conversation — a person's message, a system framing
turn, a background report, a turn the loop synthesized. Every provider has a token for it; none of
them is this datum."
data user_role()

@"Whose turn this is: the MODEL's own — text it produced on an earlier step and that is replayed to it
as its own words. The wire token differs per provider (\"assistant\" on some APIs, \"model\" on others);
which one goes out is the provider adapter's decision, made by matching this sum."
data model_role()

// WHO spoke one turn — a closed sum, so a provider adapter must map it and an app cannot invent a
// third value. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type role = user_role | model_role

@"One turn in a conversation: who spoke (`user_role` / `model_role`), what they said, and any files
attached to it (a chat attachment, a generated image). Files ride as blob handles; each provider decides
when to inline their content for the model (see the providers' inline policy). @author@ is the
OPTIONAL opaque end-user tag of a user turn (e.g. a hashed chat-user id) — never shown to the model;
a provider whose API has an abuse-attribution field lifts the newest user turn's author into it
(`ai.attribution`). `null` (the default) means the turn is not attributable to an end user: a system
framing turn, a monitor notification, a loop-synthesized turn."
data turn(role: role, text: string, files: array[file] ?= [], author: string | null ?= null)

@"THE correlation handle of one tool call: a single OPAQUE token, minted by the provider that parsed
the model's pick and replayed verbatim on the call turn AND on the result that answers it. WHAT it
holds is the provider's own business — an id its wire keys a result by, or a token its wire demands
echoed back on the call — and nothing above the provider ever reads it. It exists so a result and its
call correlate by carrying the SAME value, which is one channel rather than a choice between two
mutually exclusive keys."
data call_ref(@"The provider's opaque per-call token, replayed verbatim." token: string)

@"One tool the model wants to invoke this step — @name@ is its function name (the dispatch key and the
model-facing call/response record), @ref@ is the call's opaque correlation handle, @args@ is the model's
argument object as a parsed value (replayed verbatim into the call turn, and dispatched straight
through `reflection.call_agent`, whose delegation boundary unconditionally turns any `$katari_ref` the
model replayed into the `file` it denotes)."
data tool_call(name: string, ref: call_ref, args: unknown)

@"A tool's output for the model, carrying the @ref@ of the call it answers so it round-trips as a
function response, and that call's tool @name@ so the record stays readable (and so a wire that keys
its responses by name has the name to hand). @files@ are the `file` values found in the result (a
screenshot an MCP tool returned, an edited image): @text@ already shows their `$katari_ref` handles
(the result's wire form), and a provider that can additionally inline their content does so while the
result is fresh."
data tool_result(name: string, ref: call_ref, text: string, files: array[file])

@"A conversation turn recording the model's tool-call batch — replayed so the next step shows the model what it already asked for."
data call_turn(calls: array[tool_call])

@"A conversation turn carrying the results of a tool-call batch."
data result_turn(results: array[tool_result])

// One conversation-history element. Each provider maps it to whatever its API calls content, so the
// function-calling loop closes. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type message = turn | call_turn | result_turn

@"One inference step's decision: a final reply text for the user."
data step_final(text: string)

@"One inference step's decision: invoke a batch of tools (dispatched in parallel, results fed back together)."
data step_call(calls: array[tool_call])

// One inference step's decision. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type step = step_final | step_call

@"One step's token accounting, as the provider's API reported it. @input_tokens@ counts the whole
context the step consumed (every provider re-reads the full history each step), so the FRESHEST
step's figure IS the session's current context occupancy — figures across steps do not add.
@output_tokens@ counts what the model generated on the step."
data usage(input_tokens: integer, output_tokens: integer)

@"What a provider returns for one model step: the decision plus its token accounting."
data step_result(step: step, usage: usage)

@"What a provider returns for one structured-output step: the decoded object (the wire's best effort
at the requested schema — `ai.infer_structured` validates it as T) plus the step's token accounting."
data object_result(value: unknown, usage: usage)
